You've STILL missed the point.

The PC can have a food item in inventory, which is
ALREADY ROTTED; inventory contains no indication of
rottedness status.

The PC can then pick up a food item of the same type
which is _nearly_ rotted, see it unmerged with the
already rotted item in inventory because they have
different status, drop the nearly rotted item, pick
it up again, only to find that its rottedness
timeout has now completed in those two turns, and it
is fully rotted.

The PC will therefore see the second item merge in
inventory the second time it is picked up, even
though it didn't merge in inventory the first time
it is picked up.

This matches the OP's description of events seen,
and _also_ corresponds to the existing code both in
behavior _and_ in intent.

This is also going to be a fairly rare event, and one
rarely noticed by a person not watching out for that
explicit event happening. Who cares, after all,
unless inventory slots are in very scarce supply,
whether inventory shows two or three different slots
containing "food rations", since it is often the
case that inventory shows more than one such slot.

This is also not going to be revealed by testing
which does not result in rottedness timeouts in
exactly the proper situation, something your testing
failed to accomplish, and which would probably be
quite difficult to accomplish in wizard mode, since
rottedness timeout is invisible for items in
inventory as well as for items on the ground.

As I posted last time, you _really_ have to
understand the data situations which the code can
encounter, before claiming that you "know" how the
code will work or does work.

To programmers, that thinking process leads to "edge
testing" or "testing corner cases". Doing it for a
living for decades (as I have) makes it habitual
behavior.

